# Every regime a filing declares for itself. One row per `pm:Regime` in a filing: its handle, its
# jurisdiction, and the framework and chart it reports under, each a borrowed term or the typed
# reason there is none.
#
# Tall, because one filing may report under two
#   `refutation` reports one microentity to two authorities, `NC-ME` to one and `M` to the other,
#   and the pair is that document's subject. So no row is "the" regime of a filing: `seq` keeps
#   the declarations apart in document order and `id` is the handle a part names.
#
# A term is whole or absent
#   A framework is a taxonomy and a value, and the value alone is the ambiguous half: SAF-T PT's
#   `S` and IES's `NCRF-PE` come from lists that do not line up. The schema keeps every term whole,
#   so "this filing states a framework" is `framework_value IS NOT NULL` and asking it of the
#   taxonomy gives the same rows. Every reader here spells it the one way.
-- pm:Regime, one row per declaration in document order.
SELECT r.filing, r.seq, r.id, r.jurisdiction,
       r.framework_taxonomy, r.framework_value, r.framework_absent,
       r.chart_taxonomy, r.chart_value, r.chart_absent
FROM pm.regime r
